{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015iXQMQ2RjJAr3AefrtaXh7","type":"tool_result","content":" *\n * WARNING: The data in the ZSTD_inBuffer in the range [src, src + pos) MUST\n * not be modified during compression or it will result in data corruption.\n * This is because zstd needs to reference data in the ZSTD_inBuffer to find\n * matches. Normally zstd maintains its own window buffer for this purpose,\n * but passing this flag tells zstd to rely on user provided buffer instead.\n */\n#define ZSTD_c_stableInBuffer ZSTD_c_experimentalParam9\n\n/* ZSTD_c_stableOutBuffer\n * Experimental parameter.\n * Default is 0 == disabled. Set to 1 to enable.\n *\n * Tells he compressor that the ZSTD_outBuffer will not be resized between\n * calls. Specifically: (out.size - out.pos) will never grow. This gives the\n * compressor the freedom to say: If the compressed data doesn't fit in the\n * output buffer then return ZSTD_error_dstSizeTooSmall. This allows us to\n * always decompress directly into the output buffer, instead of decompressing\n * into an internal buffer and copying to the output buffer.\n *\n * When this flag is enabled zstd won't allocate an output buffer, because\n * it can write directly to the ZSTD_outBuffer. It will still allocate the\n * input window buffer (see ZSTD_c_stableInBuffer).\n *\n * Zstd will check that (out.size - out.pos) never grows and return an error\n * if it does. While not strictly necessary, this should prevent surprises.\n */\n#define ZSTD_c_stableOutBuffer ZSTD_c_experimentalParam10\n\n/* ZSTD_c_blockDelimiters\n * Default is 0 == ZSTD_sf_noBlockDelimiters.\n *\n * For use with sequence compression API: ZSTD_compressSequences().\n *\n * Designates whether or not the given array of ZSTD_Sequence contains block delimiters\n * and last literals, which are defined as sequences with offset == 0 and matchLength == 0.\n * See the definition of ZSTD_Sequence for more specifics.\n */\n#define ZSTD_c_blockDelimiters ZSTD_c_experimentalParam11\n\n/* ZSTD_c_validateSequences\n * Default is 0 == disabled. Set to 1 to enable sequence validation.\n *\n * For use with sequence compression API: ZSTD_compressSequences*().\n * Designates whether or not provided sequences are validated within ZSTD_compressSequences*()\n * during function execution.\n *\n * When Sequence validation is disabled (default), Sequences are compressed as-is,\n * so they must correct, otherwise it would result in a corruption error.\n *\n * Sequence validation adds some protection, by ensuring that all values respect boundary conditions.\n * If a Sequence is detected invalid (see doc/zstd_compression_format.md for\n * specifics regarding offset/matchlength requirements) then the function will bail out and\n * return an error.\n */\n=== d_stableOutBuffer ===\n2503- * Experimental parameter.\n2504- * Default is 0 == disabled. Set to 1 to enable.\n2505- *\n2506- * Tells the decompressor that the ZSTD_outBuffer will ALWAYS be the same\n2507- * between calls, except for the modifications that zstd makes to pos (the\n2508- * caller must not modify pos). This is checked by the decompressor, and\n2509- * decompression will fail if it ever changes. Therefore the ZSTD_outBuffer\n2510- * MUST be large enough to fit the entire decompressed frame. This will be\n2511- * checked when the frame content size is known. The data in the ZSTD_outBuffer\n2512- * in the range [dst, dst + pos) MUST not be modified during decompression\n2513- * or you will get data corruption.\n2514- *\n2515- * When this flag is enabled zstd won't allocate an output buffer, because\n2516- * it can write directly to the ZSTD_outBuffer, but it will still allocate\n2517- * an input buffer large enough to fit any compressed block. This will also\n2518- * avoid the memcpy() from the internal output buffer to the ZSTD_outBuffer.\n2519- * If you need to avoid the input buffer allocation use the buffer-less\n2520- * streaming API.\n2521- *\n2522- * NOTE: So long as the ZSTD_outBuffer always points to valid memory, using\n2523- * this flag is ALWAYS memory safe, and will never access out-of-bounds\n2524- * memory. However, decompression WILL fail if you violate the preconditions.\n2525- *\n2526- * WARNING: The data in the ZSTD_outBuffer in the range [dst, dst + pos) MUST\n2527- * not be modified during decompression or you will get data corruption. This\n2528- * is because zstd needs to reference data in the ZSTD_outBuffer to regenerate\n2529- * matches. Normally zstd maintains its own buffer for this purpose, but passing\n2530- * this flag tells zstd to use the user provided buffer.\n2531- */\n2532:#define ZSTD_d_stableOutBuffer ZSTD_d_experimentalParam2\n2533-\n2534-/* ZSTD_d_forceIgnoreChecksum\n2535- * Experimental parameter.\n2536- * Default is 0 == disabled. Set to 1 to enable\n2537- *\n2538- * Tells the decompressor to skip checksum validation during decompression, regardless\n2539- * of whether checksumming was specified during compression. This offers some\n2540- * slight performance benefits, and may be useful for debugging.\n2541- * Param has values of type ZSTD_forceIgnoreChecksum_e\n2542- */\n2543-#define ZSTD_d_forceIgnoreChecksum ZSTD_d_experimentalParam3\n2544-\n2545-/* ZSTD_d_refMultipleDDicts\n2546- * Experimental parameter.\n2547- * Default is 0 == disabled. Set to 1 to enable","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01YNXnHPsGBqwCpksc3jfqzQ","session_id":"f3f9022d-a72c-4fdb-a0a0-2ee15f7b56e5","uuid":"0ad5b990-a26c-4a63-b926-0a11d872217c","timestamp":"2026-08-14T21:34:26.279Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write streaming and block-level tests"}
