uildDTable_wksp → decompress_wksp_bmi2`, with full CTable/DTable/NCount buffers compared and round-trip identity asserted; plus 500 synthetic random histograms and 160 random sources.\n- **HUF** — all 27 exported entry points, full CTable (`u64[257]`) / DTable (`u32[4097]`) / stream bytes compared, `HUF_repeat_none|check|valid` transitions, X1+X2 decoders, all 6 `HUF_flags_e`, `HUF_BLOCKSIZE_MAX` payload, 500 synthetic histograms.\n- **HIST**, **xxhash** (incl. every length 0..=512, unaligned starts, streaming with 1-/7-/random-byte chunk plans, `copyState` mid-stream, canonical BE round-trip), **POOL** stub + `ZSTD_CCtx_refThreadPool`.\n- **`entropy_corrupt_payloads`** — ~450 truncated/bit-flipped/noise payloads pushed into every deserialiser (`FSE_readNCount`, `FSE_decompress_wksp_bmi2`, `HUF_readStats(_wksp)`, `HUF_readCTable`, `HUF_readDTableX1/X2_wksp`, all four `HUF_decompress*_DCtx_wksp`, `ZDICT_getDictID`/`getDictHeaderSize`).\n\n## Findings\n\n**No divergence survives in the defined-behaviour domain** — the Rust port is byte-identical to the C for every in-contract input tested.\n\nThree real C-vs-Rust divergences *were* observed during development, but all are confined to inputs that violate a documented precondition, so I narrowed the input domain rather than assert on undefined behaviour (all three are worth knowing about, none is a port bug):\n\n1. `FSE_optimalTableLog_internal(maxTableLog=0, srcSize=1, maxSymbolValue=0, minus=0)` → **C=5, Rust=11**. Root cause: `ZSTD_highbit32((U32)(srcSize-1))` with argument 0; `bits.h:176` is `assert(val != 0)` and the function itself carries `assert(srcSize > 1)`. Same for `srcSize=0`. Test now sweeps `srcSize >= 2`.\n2. `HUF_minTableLog(0)` → **C=1, Rust=0**. Same `ZSTD_highbit32(0)` cause; cardinality 0 cannot come from a real histogram. Test now sweeps `card >= 1`.\n3. `HUF_buildCTable_wksp` / `HUF_setMaxHeight` **SIGSEGVs in both libs** when `tableLog < HUF_minTableLog(cardinality)` (e.g. `tableLog=5` with a 256-symbol alphabet — fewer codes than symbols). Guarded with the same `HUF_minTableLog` check zstd itself relies on.\n\nOne upstream quirk the Rust port faithfully reproduces, flagged for awareness: `FSE_BUILD_CTABLE_WORKSPACE_SIZE_U32` truncates `((maxSymbolValue+2)+(1<<tableLog))/2`, so for **odd `maxSymbolValue`** (e.g. 255) the macro under-reports by 2 bytes while `FSE_buildCTable_wksp`'s `spread` buffer writes 8 bytes at a time past `tableSize`. Harmless in real zstd (the workspace is a slice of the much larger `ZSTD_cwksp`), but it corrupts an exactly-sized caller allocation. Both libs overrun identically; the test allocates `SLACK_U32` extra while still passing the exact macro-computed `wkspSize`.","usage":{"total_tokens":277587,"tool_uses":110,"duration_ms":2149436},"uuid":"70a4faa7-596b-44ee-9df3-142016de1de9","session_id":"f3f9022d-a72c-4fdb-a0a0-2ee15f7b56e5"}
